From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] The client gives a buffer's range back before the owner can hand it out, and places direct memory only

A buffer of the owner's shared memory (its direct memory refused, or PROTON_GPU_HOST_DMEM=0) was laid back to a
reservation only after FREE returned. The owner gives the range back at once (HostRange::take is first fit), so another
thread's ALLOC could get the same address in between; its query read the old shared mapping as the bare reservation
under it (the kernel does not list physical shared memory there), it mapped its buffer, possibly wired direct memory
(0x33), and the freeing thread then laid a reservation over that live buffer, a mapping it did not make, or unmapped
it. It also took and closed that thread's source entry. Every host-visible buffer is now laid back (or unmapped where
the lay fails) and its source dropped before FREE is sent, as direct memory already was; only the counters follow it.

A placed mapping (VK_EXT_map_memory_placed, a WoW64 program's) of shared memory is refused: over the reservation
PS5_Proton's win32u hands over (Wine patch 1130) such a mapping reads as that reservation, so Wine's direct-memory
engine took the range for its own and a VirtualProtect there mapped fresh direct memory over the GPU-visible pages.
Wired direct memory it reads as a mapping not its own. So the client keeps no descriptor any more, only the
direct-memory offsets, and only when the application asked (ps5_gpu_client_keep_sources); a shared-memory buffer
fails its placed map (VK_ERROR_MEMORY_MAP_FAILED) instead.

Host: tests/test_crash_replays.c runs this client against tests/fake_ps5_kernel.c ("GPU client free order": a
shared-memory buffer freed while another thread's ALLOC gets its address, which the old order tore down;
"placed shared memory": refused, and Wine's commit there maps nothing over it). Console: not run.

Base: PS5_Vulkan f410e67a + 0001-0013.
